## MCP routing and loop control

When the user explicitly requests MCP-only execution or names a specific MCP server, use MCP direct tools, `mcp`, and `mcpScript` for that part of the task. This explicit request overrides generic browser-tool preferences; when Playwright MCP is requested, do not substitute Chrome DevTools.

For two or more independent read-only MCP checks, prefer one `mcpScript` batch and execute the independent calls concurrently when supported. Do not serialize independent checks through separate model turns.

If the MCP server is already known, route directly to that server or list it once. Do not use one broad search query containing many unrelated intents. When discovery is required, search one narrow capability at a time, such as `router dhcp`, `nas storage`, `minidlna`, `docker status`, or `playwright navigate`.

After a tool has been discovered successfully, reuse its canonical name instead of rediscovering it.

If multiple tools that share the same backend fail with the same deterministic transport error, treat that as one shared backend blocker. Do not repeat equivalent calls through that backend. Record the blocker once and continue all independent requirements that do not depend on it.

Match each explicit requirement to the most specific available tool. Do not substitute a general status tool when a specific tool exists; for example, NAS storage requires the storage-status tool rather than the general NAS-status tool.

For Playwright MCP navigation, perform targeted discovery for `playwright_browser_navigate` when it is not already exposed. Never invent a Playwright tool name.

When a task has independent local, router/NAS, Docker, and browser checks, failure of one backend must not prevent completion of the others.
